# Unified Show Director Rules

Use these rules when editing or adding Show Director features.

## Core Boundary

The Show Director is a typed orchestration layer above existing systems. It does not replace visualization rendering, DMX output, recording, protocol handling, or project packaging.

## Required Shape

- Define stable Codable models for shows, setlists, songs, sections, cues, endpoint actions, presets, runtime state, endpoint health, and execution logs.
- Keep the timeline endpoint-neutral.
- Keep cue source separate from cue execution.
- Use a pure reducer for state transitions.
- Use an actor-isolated execution engine for endpoint I/O.
- Use endpoint adapters to call existing app services.

## Reducer Constraints

Reducers must not:

- Call DMX output.
- Start video playback.
- Touch OBS WebSocket.
- Read or write files.
- Send MIDI or OSC.
- Depend on clocks except through injected values.
- Depend on SwiftUI view state.

Reducers may:

- Validate current timeline position.
- Decide next target state.
- Produce effects for the execution engine.
- Record expected endpoint targets.

## Endpoint Adapter Constraints

Adapters should:

- Validate actions before execution.
- Return structured results.
- Report endpoint health.
- Time out or fail cleanly.
- Avoid crashing the whole show on one endpoint failure.

## UI Constraints

Setlist UI must prioritize operator confidence:

- Current cue.
- Next cue.
- Clear GO button.
- Hold/resume.
- Jump.
- Undo.
- Park.
- Blackout.
- Endpoint health.
- Manual preset browser.

Do not bury safety controls.

## Remote Protocol

Remote clients send semantic commands. They do not own runtime state.

Use command IDs for idempotency and execution logs.

Emergency actions must be explicit:

- lighting blackout
- video blackout
- stop movement
- kill strobe
- restore safe look
